'''bathmic''' (bath′mik), ''a.'' [< Gr. βαθμός (see ''bathmism'') + ''-ic.''] Pertaining to or of the nature of bathmism.
{{smaller block|I compared the transmission of ''bathmic'' force to that of the phenomenon of combustion, which is a force conversion transmitted from substance to substance by contact.
{{right|''E. D. Cope,'' Origin of the Fittest, p. 229.}}}}

'''bathmism''' (bath′mizm), ''n.'' [< Gr. βαθμός, also βασμός, a step, threshold (< βαίνειν (√ *βα), go), + ''-ism.''] See extract.
{{smaller block|It is here left open whether there be any form of force which may be especially designated as “vital.” Many of the animal functions are known to be physical and chemical, and if there be any one which appears to be less explicable by reference to these forces than the others, it is that of nutrition. Probably in this instance force has been so metamorphosed through the influence of the originative or conscious force in evolution, that it is a distinct species in the category of forces. Assuming it to be such, I have given it the name of ''Bathmism.''
